// [subject, question, answer, first review date]. Cards appear in Revise when their topic week starts.
const CARDS_ = [
  ['Java', 'How does HashMap work internally?', 'It is an array of buckets. put() spreads the key\'s hashCode (h ^ h>>>16) and picks bucket (n-1) & hash. Collisions are chained in a linked list, which turns into a red-black tree when one bucket passes 8 entries (and the table has at least 64 buckets). When size exceeds capacity x load factor (0.75), the table doubles and entries are redistributed. get/put are O(1) on average and O(log n) in a treeified bucket. Keys need consistent equals/hashCode, and mutating a key after insertion makes it unfindable.', '2026-11-02'],
  ['Java', 'What is the equals() / hashCode() contract, and what breaks if you override only one?', 'Equal objects must return the same hashCode; unequal objects may collide. If you override equals but not hashCode, two equal objects land in different buckets, so a HashSet keeps both and a HashMap lookup misses. Override both from the same fields (Objects.equals, Objects.hash).', '2026-11-02'],
  ['Java', 'Why is String immutable in Java?', 'Strings are shared through the String pool, so mutation would change every holder. Immutability also makes them thread-safe without locks, lets the hash be cached (fast HashMap keys), and keeps validated values like file paths, URLs and class names from changing after a security check. For repeated concatenation use StringBuilder.', '2026-11-02'],
  ['Java', 'Checked vs unchecked exceptions: when do you use which?', 'Checked exceptions (subclasses of Exception but not RuntimeException) must be caught or declared; use them for recoverable conditions the caller should handle, like IOException. Unchecked exceptions (RuntimeException) signal programming errors such as NullPointerException or IllegalArgumentException. Errors (OutOfMemoryError) are JVM problems you do not catch. Note: Spring rolls back @Transactional on unchecked exceptions by default.', '2026-11-02'],
  ['Java', 'ArrayList vs LinkedList: which do you use and why?', 'ArrayList is a resizable array: O(1) random access, amortised O(1) append, O(n) insert in the middle, cache friendly. LinkedList is doubly linked: O(1) insert or remove at a known node but O(n) access and more memory per element. In practice ArrayList almost always wins; for a queue or stack use ArrayDeque.', '2026-11-09'],
  ['Java', 'What are the JVM memory areas?', 'Heap: all objects, shared by threads, split into young (Eden + survivors) and old generations. Stack: one per thread, holding frames with local variables and partial results. Metaspace: class metadata in native memory (replaced PermGen in Java 8). Also the PC register and native method stack per thread. Deep recursion gives StackOverflowError; a full heap gives OutOfMemoryError.', '2026-11-16'],
  ['Java', 'How does garbage collection work in Java?', 'Objects unreachable from GC roots (thread stacks, static fields, JNI refs) are garbage. Most objects die young, so the heap is generational: frequent minor GCs on the young generation, survivors get promoted to the old generation, which is collected less often. G1 (default since Java 9) collects region by region against a pause-time goal; ZGC targets very low pauses for large heaps. System.gc() is only a hint.', '2026-11-16'],
  ['OOP', 'Abstract class vs interface: how do you choose?', 'An interface is a contract: a class can implement many, and since Java 8 they can have default and static methods but no instance state. An abstract class can hold state and constructors and share code, but a class extends only one. Use an interface for a capability (Comparable, PaymentMethod); use an abstract class for a family that shares implementation, for example with the Template Method pattern.', '2026-11-02'],
  ['OOP', 'Explain the Open/Closed principle with an example.', 'Code should be open for extension and closed for modification. Checkout depends on a PaymentStrategy interface; card, UPI and wallet are separate classes. Adding a new payment method means adding a class, not editing checkout, so tested code stays untouched. It is achieved through polymorphism, usually Strategy or Factory.', '2026-11-02'],
  ['OOP', 'Why prefer composition over inheritance?', 'Inheritance couples a subclass to its parent\'s implementation, so parent changes can break children, and it forces an is-a relationship. Composition (has-a) lets you swap behaviour at runtime and test parts in isolation. Example: a Duck has a FlyBehavior instead of subclasses for every flying style.', '2026-11-02'],
  ['Threads', 'synchronized vs volatile', 'volatile guarantees visibility and ordering for one variable (a write happens-before later reads) but not atomicity, so count++ on a volatile is still a race. synchronized gives mutual exclusion plus visibility for a whole block and is reentrant. Use volatile for flags, AtomicInteger for counters, and locks for compound actions.', '2026-11-30'],
  ['Threads', 'Why use a thread pool (ExecutorService) instead of new Thread()?', 'Creating threads is expensive and unbounded threads exhaust memory and CPU. A pool reuses threads, bounds concurrency, queues extra tasks, returns results through Future, and manages shutdown. ThreadPoolExecutor is tuned with core size, max size, keep-alive, the work queue and a rejection policy. For blocking I/O at scale, Java 21 virtual threads are an option.', '2026-11-30'],
  ['Threads', 'What is a deadlock and how do you prevent it?', 'Two or more threads each wait for a lock the other holds. All four Coffman conditions are needed: mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait. Break one: acquire locks in a fixed global order, use tryLock with a timeout, take all locks at once, or shrink lock scope. Diagnose with a thread dump (jstack).', '2026-11-30'],
  ['OS', 'Process vs thread', 'A process has its own address space and resources, so it is isolated but costly to create and switch. Threads live inside a process and share its heap, code and files while each has its own stack and registers, so they are cheap to create and switch but need synchronization. One thread crashing can bring down the whole process.', '2026-11-23'],
  ['OS', 'What happens during a context switch?', 'The OS saves the running thread\'s registers, program counter and stack pointer into its PCB/TCB, marks it ready or blocked, picks the next one with the scheduler, and restores that one\'s context. Switching between processes also switches the page table, which flushes TLB entries, so a process switch costs more than a thread switch.', '2026-11-23'],
  ['OS', 'Explain paging and virtual memory.', 'Each process sees its own virtual address space, which can be larger than RAM. Memory is split into fixed-size pages mapped to physical frames through a page table, with the TLB caching recent translations. Touching a page that is not in RAM causes a page fault and the OS loads it from disk, evicting another page (LRU, FIFO, clock). Thrashing happens when the working sets do not fit in RAM.', '2026-12-07'],
  ['OS', 'Mutex vs semaphore', 'A mutex has an owner: one thread enters the critical section and only that thread unlocks it. A semaphore is a counter of permits with no owner: it allows N concurrent users (counting) or is used for signalling between threads (binary). Example: a pool of 10 database connections is a counting semaphore.', '2026-12-07'],
  ['DBMS', 'Explain ACID with an example.', 'Bank transfer of 100 from A to B. Atomicity: both the debit and the credit happen or neither does (undo log). Consistency: constraints such as no negative balance hold before and after. Isolation: concurrent transfers behave as if run one at a time, to the degree the isolation level promises. Durability: once committed, it survives a crash (write-ahead log).', '2026-12-14'],
  ['DBMS', 'What are the isolation levels and which anomalies does each allow?', 'Read Uncommitted allows dirty reads. Read Committed stops dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads. Repeatable Read stops those but the standard still allows phantoms (MySQL InnoDB mostly prevents them with next-key locks). Serializable prevents all three. Defaults: PostgreSQL uses Read Committed, MySQL InnoDB uses Repeatable Read.', '2026-12-14'],
  ['DBMS', 'How does an index work, and when does it hurt?', 'Usually a B+ tree on the indexed columns: O(log n) lookups and fast range scans along linked leaves. A clustered index stores the rows in key order (the InnoDB primary key); secondary indexes store the primary key and need a second lookup. A composite index follows the leftmost-prefix rule. Indexes slow down writes, use storage, help little on low-selectivity columns, and are skipped when you wrap the column in a function.', '2026-12-14'],
  ['DBMS', 'What is normalization, and up to which normal form would you go?', 'Normalization removes redundancy and update anomalies. 1NF: atomic values. 2NF: no partial dependency on part of a composite key. 3NF: no transitive dependency on non-key columns. BCNF: every determinant is a candidate key. Most OLTP schemas aim for 3NF or BCNF, then denormalize on purpose for read-heavy paths.', '2026-12-21'],
  ['CN', 'What happens when you type a URL and press Enter?', 'The browser parses the URL and checks caches, then DNS resolves the domain (browser, OS, resolver, then root, TLD and authoritative servers). It opens a TCP connection (three-way handshake) and a TLS handshake that verifies the certificate and agrees keys. It sends the HTTP request, which passes a load balancer or reverse proxy to an app server that may hit caches and databases. The response comes back and the browser parses HTML, fetches CSS and JS, builds the DOM and CSSOM, and renders.', '2026-12-28'],
  ['CN', 'TCP vs UDP', 'TCP is connection-oriented, reliable and ordered, with flow and congestion control; it costs a handshake and some latency. Used by HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 and database connections. UDP is connectionless with no delivery or order guarantees and little overhead. Used by DNS, video calls, games, and QUIC, which builds reliability on top of UDP for HTTP/3.', '2026-12-28'],
  ['Spring', 'What is dependency injection and how does Spring do it?', 'Objects receive their dependencies instead of creating them (inversion of control). Spring scans @Component, @Service and @Repository classes, creates beans in its container, and wires them, preferably through constructor injection, which keeps fields final and makes testing with mocks easy. It also manages scopes (singleton by default, prototype, request) and lifecycle callbacks.', '2027-01-04'],
  ['Spring', 'How does @Transactional work, and what are its traps?', 'Spring wraps the bean in a proxy that opens a transaction before the method and commits after it, rolling back on unchecked exceptions and Errors (checked ones only with rollbackFor). Traps: calling a @Transactional method from the same class bypasses the proxy, so no transaction starts; by default it only applies to public methods. Propagation (REQUIRED by default, REQUIRES_NEW) and isolation can be set per method.', '2027-01-11'],
  ['HLD', 'Explain the CAP theorem.', 'During a network partition a distributed store must choose between consistency (every read sees the latest write) and availability (every request gets a response). Partitions will happen, so the real choice is CP (ZooKeeper, HBase) or AP (Cassandra, DynamoDB with tunable consistency). PACELC adds that even without a partition you trade latency against consistency.', '2026-12-14'],
  ['HLD', 'What is consistent hashing and why is it used?', 'Nodes and keys are hashed onto the same ring and each key belongs to the next node clockwise. Adding or removing a node moves only about K/N keys instead of reshuffling almost everything, as hash mod N would. Virtual nodes spread the load evenly. Used in DynamoDB, Cassandra and distributed caches.', '2026-12-14'],
  ['HR', 'Tell me about yourself (product SWE version)', 'Shape, 60 to 90 seconds. Present: final-year CSE, building backend and systems software in Java, Spring Boot and Go. Proof: CFC_Backend (Spring Security with JWT, Redis, S3/SQS), a sandboxed code runner in Go, merged open-source PRs in Rust, [N] LeetCode problems. Past: robotics work at IIT Delhi and on Perceptron taught you to debug real systems end to end. Future: why you want product engineering at this company. Replace the brackets with your current numbers.', '2027-01-18'],
];

